  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nTrough axis and deeper moisture shifts off to the east through the \nday today, and so does the steadiest precipitation. Even still, \nsteep low level lapse rates and enough low level moisture within the \nboundary layer may produce a few instances/areas of light showers, \ngenerally across northwest lower MI. Any showery activity should \nwane by the mid afternoon hours. Winds will shift northwest today as \nwell as cold advection overspreads the region, with winds gusts of \n~20-30 mph on average through the day today. \n\nDeep trough to our north pushes south tonight. This cooler airmass \nover the relatively mild lakes will result in a few areas of lake \ninduced rain showers, mainly for eastern upper Michigan. Small \nchance for a waterspout as well, based on the International Centre \nfor Waterspout Research. Temperatures flirt with the 30s most areas, \nalthough the degree to which the boundary layer remains mixed will \nultimately dictate how low the temperatures drop. Frost temperature \ncriteria may be possible tonight, although elevated winds may retard \nany significant frost formation. Collaboration with GRR has pushed \nany frost headlines/products for the day crew to assess further. In \nreality, the better and more widespread potential seems to be Monday \nnight. \n\nDeep trough moves to the east on Monday, with any showers moving to \nthe east as high pressure slides to the east. Sfc high pressure \nslides over northern MI Monday night, setting the stage for \naggressive radiational cooling and the potential for the coldest \nnight of the fall season. Current forecast has temperatures in the \nupper 20s to mid 30s across a good chunk of the region away from the \nimmediate Lake Michigan shoreline (marine layer/cloud/wind \ncontributions keep LM shoreline milder). Expect frost/freeze \nheadlines in the future unless something changes. \n\nThe low pressure system across the Dakotas will move eastward into \nTuesday, perhaps clipping the region with warm advection rain \nshowers Tuesday - Tuesday night. More importantly, the pressure \ngradient between this low pressure system and the aforementioned sfc \nhigh pressure system will result in breezy conditions, with another \nround of marine products.\n\nEnergy impinging from the north mid week will result in a few \nchances for showers, generally of the lake induced variety, and \nfavoring the farther north one is. Daily high temperatures do not \nfluctuate all that much, with low to mid 60s most days. It does \nappear next weekend will be dry, and pretty mild.",
